## Key Data-Quality Observations & Caveats

**GWP basis caveat**
The H1'FY25 deck reports a single GWP figure (**₹3,241.5 Cr**), with the explicit note "No Reinsurance Accepted, so GWP = GDPI." It does **not** show a dual "with/without 1/n" split — that distinction only appears in the later FY25 full-year (Q4'FY25) presentation (GWP without 1/n = ₹7,407 Cr vs. with 1/n = ₹6,762 Cr for FY25). Since H1'FY25 predates the dual-basis disclosure convention, the ₹3,241.5 Cr figure is reported here as the period's statutory GWP as originally disclosed; flag this if benchmarking against later FY25 full-year GWP figures.

**Cross-verification success**
NWP (**₹2,544.9 Cr**), NEP (**₹2,231.2 Cr**), Net worth (**₹2,064.6 Cr**), and AUM/Investments (**₹5,965.2 Cr**) all reconcile almost exactly between the Investor Presentation and the IRDAI statutory public disclosure (September 30, 2024 filing) — a strong consistency check on data integrity.

**PAT divergence — IGAAP vs. IFRS**
PAT was a **loss of ₹5.8 Cr under IGAAP** but a **profit of ₹59.5 Cr under IFRS** for H1FY25 — a swing of roughly ₹65 Cr, driven primarily by IFRS-17 insurance contract adjustments (+₹96.5 Cr), partly offset by income tax (IAS-12: -₹23.4 Cr) and share-based payment (IFRS-2: -₹7.2 Cr) adjustments. This materially changes the profitability narrative depending on which GAAP basis is used — this should be flagged prominently in any profitability comparison (e.g., against peers reporting under IGAAP only).

**EoM ratio ambiguity**
Two different EoM ratios exist across the source documents:
- "EoM as % of GWP" = **40.0%** (Investor Presentation)
- "EoM to Net Written Premium Ratio" = **50.95%** (statutory analytical ratios)

The GWP-based ratio is reported here as it aligns with IRDAI's regulatory EOM ceiling test; the NWP-based ratio uses a distinct denominator and should not be conflated with it.

**EOM regulatory overhang (risk flag)**
The statutory filing explicitly discloses that Niva Bupa exceeded the allowable EOM limit for FY23 and FY24, has filed for regulatory forbearance, and received an IRDAI show-cause notice (dated 2024-10-18) to which it responded on 2024-11-01 — outcome pending at the time of filing. **₹162.05 Cr** of excess EOM was transferred from the Revenue Account to the P&L Account for H1FY25. This is a material regulatory/compliance risk not visible in the headline ratios alone.

**Combined ratio remains above 100%**
The combined ratio of **103.5%** for H1FY25 (improved from 104.7% in H1FY24) indicates the underwriting business remains loss-making on a standalone basis; where profitability is positive (under IFRS), it is being driven by investment income and reserve/contract accounting adjustments rather than core underwriting performance.

**Commission ratio not disclosed**
Neither source publishes a named "Commission ratio" — only the absolute net commission expense (~₹508.3 Cr for H1FY25) is available. Per data-integrity rules, this was left blank rather than estimated.

**IPO/listing context**
Per statutory filing note 3, Niva Bupa completed its IPO (₹74/share) and listed on NSE/BSE on **2024-11-14** — i.e., subsequent to the September 30, 2024 balance sheet date. This means the H1FY25 figures above reflect pre-IPO, private-company financials.</ans>
